392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 191 0 R
/E (Luring teaches a dog to follow the handler's hand with confidence and trust. It is one of the most fundamental skills in training, and also one of the most misunderstood. Many dog owners treat luring as a shortcut: moving food in front of their dog's nose in the shape of a trick. But real luring is about communication. It teaches the dog that the hand is a guide, not a bribe.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 191 0 R
/E (Done well, luring builds a dog who moves willingly, trusts the handler's hand movements, and understands that following that guidance always leads somewhere good. It is simple, clear, and confidence-building, making it an excellent tool for beginner dogs and handlers alike, and far more than just a way to teach tricks. At its core, it's a genuine form of conversation between dog and handler.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 191 0 R
/E (The key is patience: move slowly, let the dog succeed, and always mark the moment they follow, so they know exactly what earned the reward.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 191 0 R
/E (Beyond teaching a dog to read hand motion, proper luring helps a dog understand the difference between a Keep Going marker and a Confirmation marker. It builds confidence and allows the dog to discover new movements and positions with clarity. Most importantly, it builds trust, because the dog must believe both in the handler and in themselves while they follow.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 191 0 R
/E (21)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
