/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (The Eye Contact & Self Control game is all about accuracy, coordination, and timing. This behavior is one of the hardest things we ask of a dog, even though it feels completely natural to us, but in this game, we teach it step by step, with as little pressure as possible.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (This exercise teaches self control and visual communication at the same time. The dog learns to resist the treat in the handler's hand and wait for a decision, rather than trying to grab the reward. But the real goal goes further than that: the dog should not just give up on the treat, but look at the handler instead, choosing eye contact over food as the way to make the reward happen.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Self control is not about suppression. It is about teaching the dog that patience pays off, that waiting is not punishment, but a strategy that leads to reward. Eye contact becomes the bridge between dog and handler: when a dog looks at the handler voluntarily, they are saying, I am with you. I am ready. Tell me what is next.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (What makes this game especially meaningful is the visual language it builds. The open hand and the closed hand become signals the dog learns to read. When the hand closes, the dog knows they made a mistake and need to change their behavior. When the hand opens, it's a sign they are on the right track, but they still need to wait for verbal confirmation before taking the treat. This connects directly to the luring language already established, adding a new layer of communication between dog and handler.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Together, these skills create a dog who can think clearly in the presence of temptation, and who chooses connection over impulse.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
