/P 195 0 R
/E (Once your dog is comfortable with self control around an open hand, hold both hands closed in front of them and slowly open them apart. Reward your dog for keeping their eyes on your face rather than on the treats. When your dog understands this, you can start adding a verbal cue before opening your hands.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (From there, begin creating small distractions by moving your hands while your dog looks at you. If they look away, either start the sequence again or simply stop moving and wait for them to look back before continuing.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Keep gradually adding more distractions, and make sure to practice with empty hands too. Opening your hands with nothing in them should eventually become an eye contact cue on its own.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [10]
>>
