>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (Follow Up is a direct progression of Reinforce & Release. Same position, same commitment, but now the dog has to think, move, and stay with the handler at the same time, even when the treat is no longer there.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (In Reinforce & Release, the dog learned to hold focus between treats while moving toward the handler. Now the question becomes whether that same focus can hold out of trust, even when a treat is not coming.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
463 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (This game prepares the dog for the front position, one of the most used positions throughout training, while building mental endurance, patience, and the ability to stay focused while waiting for confirmation. Instead of rewarding the dog at every step, the handler now praises them for following in motion, using the Keep Going marker to keep them engaged without relying on treats.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
464 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (The dog learns to stay connected to the handler not because of a lure in hand, but because the pattern of reinforcement makes staying close worthwhile. This is also what makes the game valuable beyond the front position itself: it builds concentration, the ability to handle distractions, and duration of focus during movement, skills that carry into everything built from here.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
