468 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (Start by playing a few repetitions of Reinforce & Release to warm your dog up and get them into the game. Then, instead of giving an immediate reward, use your Keep Going marker to keep your dog engaged as you take one more step backwards. After that extra step, stop and use multiple Static Confirmations with a treat in every repetition, then release your dog.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
469 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (When your dog comes back from the release, play the normal Reinforce & Release version a few more times before challenging them again with Follow Up. During the Follow Up, try to hold your hands behind your back or at your sides so your dog has to think about the position in front of you, rather than following your hands.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
470 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (Slowly start increasing the duration of each repetition. Use your Keep Going marker as you walk backwards, praising your dog for staying in front of you and maintaining focus while in motion. Reward only when you give the Static Confirmation marker. Keep building the game and combine it naturally with Reinforce & Release, moving between the two so your dog learns to stay engaged through both stillness and movement.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
471 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (A few things to keep in mind)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
472 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (This game is one of the most important reads you have as a handler. Think of it this way: if your dog can follow for 10 seconds, they can hold a sequence for 5. If they can do it for 2 minutes, they are ready to dance for one. Use this game to understand where your dog actually is, not where you hope they are.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
